Warn when the engine core stops completing steps while requests are live.

The failure class in syv-ai/qwen38-27b-rtx3090#107 -- requests held, engine
alive, stepping stopped -- emits nothing: no stats line, no completion line,
no abort, no error. It is only visible today by scraping the log for a gap
between stats lines, which is what a reporter's external catcher does, and
what gave it 80+ false positives on long prefills.

A daemon thread in EngineCoreProc times the gap since the last COMPLETED step
and warns once per episode when that gap crosses VLLM_ENGINE_STALL_SENTINEL_S
(0, off, by default) while the scheduler still holds requests. Keying on
completed steps rather than on stats-line silence is what removes the
false-positive class: a chunked prefill completes a step per chunk, so it
never trips the clock, however long the prefill runs in total.

Three deviations from the draft in the reporter's gist, all deliberate:

  - the knob is registered in vllm/envs.py rather than read with os.getenv,
    per review on #107 (it also enrols the knob in the compile-cache key);
  - the sentinel thread calls get_request_counts() itself on each tick
    instead of reading a snapshot the main loop refreshes. A core that stops
    stepping with a stale non-zero snapshot -- e.g. a request parked
    WAITING_FOR_REMOTE_KVS while the loop falls through to the input queue --
    would otherwise fire against counts nobody refreshed. This also deletes
    the step_started hook entirely, so the patch touches one call site;
  - the sentinel is constructed AFTER super().__init__() rather than beside
    the `identity = ...` assignment. self.scheduler does not exist yet at
    that point in EngineCoreProc.__init__; the draft's placement aborts every
    boot with AttributeError: 'EngineCoreProc' object has no attribute
    'scheduler'. (Observed, not reasoned: 2026-09-15 08:32:21 on the
    reference 3090.)

The draft's `+import threading` is also dropped: 0.28.0's core.py already
imports threading at line 7, and the draft adds a second one in the
`from multiprocessing.queues import Queue` block, which fails ruff/isort.

Measured on the reference 3090 (0.28.0, CTX=fast, SPEC=dflash2, threshold 30 s,
one boot): zero stall lines across 20 sequential decodes, one client-SIGKILLed
stream and one 44,820-token / 28.5 s chunked prefill -- i.e. no false positive
on the arm that fires the log-scraper. No wedge was induced, so the patch's
true-positive path is unexercised here; it is the same clock the draft used.

